# The hidden helper of the library's flatMap runs over a plain list first: it asks the storage for the index, takes the element out of it, and lays a value that is no list by a store that a plain list takes.
# It asked `in` and a closed read for each element and laid each value by a definition in C++. A hole ends the run: the general loop goes on from there (the second answer).
# The run stands in the helper and not in flatMap: flatMap ends in a tail call of the helper, so no frame of flatMap stands below the callback; f5 holds no run.
# f4 = flatIntoArrayWithCallback, f5 = flatMap of the library
# -- source: flat-map-takes-the-elements-of-a-plain-list-out-of-its-storage.cjs
# const box = {};
# box['k' + [].length] = [[1, 2, 3], (x) => [x, x * 2], [4, , 6]];
# print(box.k0[0].flatMap(box.k0[1]).join(), box.k0[2].flatMap(box.k0[1]).join());
# -- end of source
# RUN: walk f4 mechanisms=all
# CHECK-LABEL: code f4:Body
# CHECK: = HasContiguousIndex
# CHECK: = GetOwnContiguousIndex
# RUN(STORE): walk f4 mechanisms=all
# STORE: = TryPutIndex
# RUN(TAIL): walk f5 mechanisms=all
# TAIL: = HasContiguousIndex
